# Map FormSy product content to one specification

Use this mapping when drafting requirements in `specify` and making
substantial changes in legacy `clarify`. Use the upstream Spec Kit
specification template and stage instructions for the document structure.
Apply the FormSy product module's requirements quality and source rules
within the same specification and workflow.

## Read the applicable resources

Use `.formsy-sop/skills/formsy-development-kit/modules/formsy-sop-product/` as the product resource root.
Read `GUIDE.md` and the resources that apply to the current object:

- `references/stages-and-sources.md` for the stage depth, sources, and treatment of unknowns.
- `references/product-fields.md` for user tasks, journeys, pages, features,
  business states, inputs, outputs, and acceptance fields.
- Use `assets/product-sop-template.en-us.md` for `en-US` or `assets/产品SOP模板.md`
  for `zh-CN` and legacy runs without a saved document locale. Incorporate only
  the material relevant to the feature scope into `spec.md`.
- `references/product-review-rules.md` for PC01–PC15 applicability, concrete
  findings, evidence, and unchecked items. Follow its frozen source link for
  original quotations.

The packaged `provenance.json` identifies the source of the included
snapshot. Record fresh reads separately. Leave technical objects, APIs, and
implementation decisions to `plan`.

## Minimum section mapping

| FormSy content | Location in spec.md | Identity and source |
| --- | --- | --- |
| User, problem, scenario, goal, scope, and priority rationale | Input, User Scenarios & Testing, and a short Scope & Sources section when needed | Preserve the original user reference. Use stable story IDs such as US1 for this feature. |
| Journey from entry point to an observable outcome, applicable page states, feature inputs and outputs, and side effects | User Story and Acceptance Scenarios, with exceptions in Edge Cases | Describe simple behavior directly. Preserve existing J/UI/F/B IDs and map them to US/FR IDs. |
| Required behavior, boundaries, permissions, data states, and applicable quality requirements | Functional Requirements, with applicable quality constraints under Requirements | Use stable IDs such as FR-001. Map existing requirement IDs and preserve them. |
| Observable acceptance results, environment, and boundaries | Given/When/Then scenarios and Success Criteria | Use IDs such as SC-001 and preserve mappings for existing acceptance IDs. Record planned checks as planned. |
| Business entities and the meaning of their states | Key Entities | Describe product semantics. Leave table structure and implementation to planning. |
| Original request, actual decisions, source snapshots, assumptions, and unresolved issues | A short Scope & Sources or Clarifications section | Cite the actual request or material and its version. Mark material ambiguities NEEDS CLARIFICATION. |
| Initial PC findings and items requiring recheck | A short Product Review section or the existing stage review report | Record PC IDs, reviewed objects, status, evidence, and problems under the existing review and approval contract. |

In `clarify`, use the upstream categories to identify questions that change
scope, acceptance, or implementation. Resolve the critical choices and
reuse existing answers. Update the related FRs, scenarios, and SCs along
with the clarification log. Resolve conflicts in the document body. When
no material ambiguity remains, record the basis and continue.

`active required` is a current constraint and `active advisory` is guidance.
`pending` and Birdview artifact rules are proposals. List conflicts between
principles, requirements, and rules. Record official activation from its
actual source. At the end of `specify/clarify`, save the complete current
draft and material revisions with their versions and sources. Review the
draft through the stage decision contract.
